  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Isolated showers are expected along and ahead of a \ncold front through most of the predawn hours of Thursday morning.\n\nW'erly flow aloft and embedded shortwave disturbances affect our\nregion through daybreak Thursday. At the surface, a high \npressure ridge continues to exit E'ward before a cold front \nsweeps E'ward through our region very late this evening through \nabout 5 AM EDT Thursday morning. Behind the front, another high \npressure ridge builds slowly from the Upper Midwest and \nvicinity. Low-level convergence/moist ascent along the front \nand pre-front surface trough axes accompanying the \naforementioned shortwave disturbances should release weak, yet \nsufficient boundary layer CAPE to trigger isolated and periodic \nshowers. Fair weather and minor clearing are expected behind \nthe front as stabilizing subsidence accompanies the aforementioned\nsurface ridge. However, low clouds should remain abundant as \nrich low-level moisture from recent rainfall becomes trapped \nbeneath the subsidence inversion and nocturnal cooling occurs \nthrough shortly after daybreak Thursday morning. The minor \nclearing and weak low-level CAA behind the front will contribute\nto lows reaching the 60's around daybreak Thursday. Sufficient \nnocturnal cooling and the rich low-level moisture should yield \nlocalized steam fog in the lake and river valleys in and near \nthe Western Southern Tier around daybreak.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Near to slightly below normal temperatures with dry \nweather to return for the end of the work week.\n\nSurface high pressure will build gradually into Western and\nCentral NY Thursday through Friday, supporting dry weather \nthrough the start of the weekend. Additionally, in the wake of \nthe passing front Thursday, a cooler airmass will slide across \nthe region for the end of the week, supporting near to slightly \nbelow normal temperatures.",
